# gitleaks suppression file: one accepted finding per line, by fingerprint.
#
# The Security workflow's secret scan (.github/scripts/secret-scan.ps1) fails on any secret gitleaks
# finds in the commit history. When a finding is a false positive or an accepted test fixture, add its
# fingerprint here, with a comment saying who accepted it, why and when, so it does not fail later runs.
# Whole files that only ever hold development values belong in the .gitleaks.toml allowlist instead.
#
# A fingerprint looks like:  <commit-sha>:<file-path>:<rule-id>:<line-number>
# gitleaks prints the exact fingerprint for each finding; copy it verbatim.
#
# Example:
#   # Test fixture, not a real key. Accepted by @worm-brain 2026-10-14.
#   0adc1e2f...:tests/Some.Tests/Fixtures/sample.json:generic-api-key:12

# Not keys: the sample test page's document id and its block's content key, GUIDs that the
# generic-api-key rule matches on the names `TEST_PAGE_KEY` and `contentKey`. Both lines were
# renamed in a later commit; these fingerprints cover the commit that added them. Added
# 2026-10-14 with the e2e suite, for the maintainer to accept by merging its pull request.
